(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-quiz"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Under the modified Duke criteria, in which situation is transesophageal echocardiography required for optimal assessment?","choices":["A stable murmur known for years","Planning dental prophylaxis","Screening every patient with fever","Possible prosthetic valve endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is required for optimal assessment of possible prosthetic valve endocarditis or complicated endocarditis. A positive echocardiogram, meaning an oscillating mass, an abscess or new partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve, is a major criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Under the modified Duke criteria, Osler nodes count as which minor criterion?","choices":["Immunologic phenomenon","Vascular phenomenon","Microbiologic evidence","Predisposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are an immunologic phenomenon, the minor criterion that also includes glomerulonephritis, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor. They are painful, which helps separate them from the painless Janeway lesions that count as vascular phenomena. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organism causes the most lethal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Gram-negative coccobacilli","Enterococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal endocarditis organism, with mortality of about 30% in people who do not inject drugs, 50% in prosthetic valve endocarditis and 5% in people who inject drugs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which consultant manages antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Addiction medicine","Palliative care","Infectious disease","Cardiothoracic surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Infectious disease is consulted for antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring pending disposition. Cardiothoracic surgery evaluates surgery and anticoagulation needs, and addiction medicine provides harm reduction for people who inject drugs, all within a multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Janeway lesions are particularly associated with which form of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Acute infective endocarditis","Subacute infective endocarditis","Non-infective (Libman-Sacks) disease","Healed, treated endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions, raised or macular hemorrhagic lesions on the palms and soles that are caused by septic emboli and are nontender, are associated with acute infective endocarditis. Their painlessness separates them from Osler nodes. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Roughly what share of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated?","choices":["Under 5%","25 to 35%","About 50%","Over 80%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About 25-35% of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated. It should be suspected in hospitalized patients who develop fever and positive blood cultures without an apparent source. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which autoimmune disease can produce a non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis that mimics infective endocarditis?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Reactive arthritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic lupus erythematosus may present with non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis, which places it in the autoimmune and rheumatologic differential of infective endocarditis. Antiphospholipid syndrome, with thromboemboli and cardiac valve disease, is its neighbor on that list. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which prophylaxis fits a penicillin-allergic patient who cannot take oral medication?","choices":["Ampicillin injection","Oral cephalexin","Clindamycin injection","Cefazolin or ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin-allergic patient who cannot take oral medication receives cefazolin or ceftriaxone 1 g intramuscularly or intravenously 30-60 minutes before the procedure. These cephalosporins should not be used if the allergy was anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin or ampicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why does a new murmur develop in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Narrowing of a coronary artery by plaque","Valve damage or chordae tendineae rupture","Increased flow from a high-output fever","Pericardial friction from inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A new cardiac murmur in infective endocarditis is due to valvular damage or rupture of the chordae tendineae. The same valvular dysfunction can lead to congestive heart failure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"When should empiric antibiotics be started for suspected infective endocarditis?","choices":["Before any cultures, to save time","Only after the echocardiogram","Only once the organism is known","Only after blood cultures are drawn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is started ONLY after blood cultures have been drawn: three sets from separate sites, or 2 cultures within the first 1-2 hours if the presentation suggests acute disease. The empiric regimen then covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which kind of antibiotic therapy does infective endocarditis require?","choices":["Bacteriostatic and lifelong","Bactericidal and prolonged","Bacteriostatic and brief","Bactericidal for 3 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All bacteria in the vegetation must be killed, so antibiotic therapy must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks from the first negative culture. It is given parenterally with hospital admission and close monitoring. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"In a child with endocarditis, a fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests which organisms?","choices":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Candida or other fungal species","Pneumococcus or Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci or enterococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Children and adolescents usually have an indolent course with prolonged low-grade fever, but a fulminant presentation with high spiking fevers suggests Streptococcus pneumoniae or Staphylococcus aureus. The abrupt, high-fever pattern is the clue. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How are the petechiae of infective endocarditis described?","choices":["Large bruises that spread over days","Firm nodules that last for months","Minute hemorrhages that can fade","Painful nodules on the finger pads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The petechiae of infective endocarditis are minute hemorrhages that can be transient. They can involve the buccal mucosa (palate), conjunctiva and skin of the extremities. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which organism causes fungal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Kingella","Eikenella","Enterococcus","Candida"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Candida is the listed fungal cause of infective endocarditis, a category separate from the bacterial causes of acute and subacute disease and from the rare aseptic cases. When a culture grows Candida, the infection is fungal endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When is the risk of prosthetic valve endocarditis greatest?","choices":["Ten or more years after replacement","During the operation itself","Equal at every point after surgery","Within the first year after replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The greatest risk of prosthetic valve endocarditis is within the first year after replacement; it then gradually declines until it is similar to the risk for a native valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which antibiotic treats infective endocarditis caused by the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group?","choices":["Nafcillin","Vancomycin","Rifampin","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone 2 g intravenously once daily for 4 weeks. The listed alternative is ampicillin-sulbactam for 4 weeks. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the role of anticoagulation in native valve infective endocarditis?","choices":["It dissolves vegetations","It is not beneficial","It prevents septic emboli","It is routinely required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is not beneficial in patients with native valve infection, and it is controversial in patients with prosthetic valves. Cardiothoracic surgery evaluates anticoagulation needs alongside the indications for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which sex is affected more often by infective endocarditis?","choices":["Men","Women","Equal in both","Women after menopause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Men are affected more often than women. Other listed epidemiologic features are older age (people over 60 account for more than 50% of cases), injection drug use, immunocompromised status, poor dentition or dental infections, and human immunodeficiency virus infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient, what is recommended about prophylaxis for transesophageal echocardiography, upper endoscopy, colonoscopy or cystoscopy?","choices":["It is not needed without infection","It is required before each one","It is needed for colonoscopy and cystoscopy","It is given as a 7-day course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In patients with valve disease at high risk of endocarditis, antibiotic prophylaxis is not recommended for nondental procedures such as transesophageal echocardiography, upper endoscopy, colonoscopy or cystoscopy in the absence of active infection. Prophylaxis is reserved for qualifying dental procedures. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is a perivalvular abscess?","choices":["Infection extending past the leaflets into the myocardium","A sterile clot sitting on a prosthetic valve sewing ring","Pus collecting in the pericardial sac after surgery","A pocket infection around a pacemaker generator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A perivalvular abscess forms when infection extends beyond the valve leaflets into the surrounding myocardial tissue. From there it can extend into the epicardium, causing pericarditis, or into the upper ventricular septum, interrupting the conduction system. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which urinalysis findings are expected in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria","Glucosuria and ketonuria","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","A completely normal sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis in infective endocarditis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria. These findings sit alongside the rest of the workup: three sets of blood cultures from separate sites, transesophageal echocardiography and a complete blood count with leukocytosis and a normocytic anemia. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is bacteremia defined?","choices":["Inflammation of the inner lining of the heart","A vegetation seen on transesophageal imaging","A fever above 38 degrees Celsius with chills","Bacteria detected on a true-positive blood culture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacteremia is the presence of bacteria in the bloodstream, identified by detection of any true-positive blood culture. It differs from endocarditis, which is inflammation of the endocardium, the inner membrane lining the heart chambers and covering the valve cusps. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How long do Osler nodes typically last?","choices":["They last minutes, like an urticarial wheal","They grow steadily until antibiotics stop","They appear in crops and fade in 2 to 3 days","They persist for months and scar the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes appear in crops and fade in 2-3 days. They are painful lesions on the palms, soles and distal phalanges, attributed to septic emboli or immune complexes with microscopic abscesses. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which prophylaxis fits a patient with no penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication?","choices":["Oral doxycycline","Intravenous vancomycin","Ampicillin by injection","Clindamycin intravenous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patient with no penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication receives ampicillin 2 g intramuscularly or intravenously within 30-60 minutes before the procedure; cefazolin or ceftriaxone is the listed alternative in this situation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition warrants endocarditis prophylaxis before dental work?","choices":["Controlled hypertension","Prior bypass grafting","Prior valve infection","Coronary artery stents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Previous infective endocarditis is one of the conditions for which prophylaxis is reasonable before dental procedures that manipulate gingival tissue or the periapical region or perforate the oral mucosa. The others are prosthetic valves, valve-repair material, certain congenital heart disease and cardiac transplant with valve regurgitation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Infective endocarditis is classified by temporal evolution, by site, by predisposing risk factor and by which other feature?","choices":["The murmur's intensity grade","The cause, such as the organism","The size of the vegetation","The degree of valve stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infective endocarditis is classified by temporal evolution (acute or subacute), site of infection (native valve, prosthetic valve or device), cause of infection (for example, methicillin-resistant or methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus) and predisposing risk factors (for example, injection drug use). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A gastrointestinal source of bacteremia points to which endocarditis organism?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Kingella species","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Streptococcus gallolyticus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus is the gastrointestinal tract organism among the causes of infective endocarditis. Each source has its organism: oral cavity, viridans streptococci; skin, staphylococci; upper respiratory tract, the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group; genitourinary tract, enterococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is preferred for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis of a native valve?","choices":["Ampicillin plus gentamicin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Vancomycin or daptomycin","Ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus is treated with nafcillin or oxacillin, or cefazolin. Susceptibility is the decisive clue, because vancomycin or daptomycin is the listed regimen for methicillin-resistant strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms cause the largest share of endocarditis on implantable electronic devices?","choices":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Enterococci"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coagulase-negative staphylococci cause the largest share of cardiac implantable electronic device endocarditis, about 41%, with Staphylococcus aureus close behind at about 36%. Together, staphylococci account for more than three quarters of device infections. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Subacute infective endocarditis is gradually progressive unless what happens?","choices":["The fever rises above 38 degrees","The murmur becomes softer","A major embolus or aneurysm rupture","The patient starts a daily aspirin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Subacute infective endocarditis is gradually progressive unless complicated by a major embolic event or a ruptured mycotic aneurysm. Otherwise it follows an indolent course: lower fevers, malaise and weight loss over weeks or months, slow structural damage and rare metastasis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
